PARS = [
("Surface fire spread is described", """The hazard model and the cost measure used in this article are standard ones. Surface fire spread is described by Rothermel-type rate-of-spread models driven by fuel, moisture, wind, and slope {7;27;28}. These models are implemented in operational tools such as FlamMap {8} and in cellular simulators such as Cell2Fire {29}. The simulation core of VODA uses a model of the same family. Decision support built on these models ranges from strategic risk assessment {4;9} and multi-criteria allocation {10;16;30} to the integrated WFDSS platform {11}, whose field use has been studied extensively {12;13;14}. In all of them the decision logic is authored offline and the intervention menu is fixed. Fire management economics judges a response by the sum of two terms, the cost of the resources committed and the loss that still occurs {3;31}. The decision cost of VODA follows the same structure."""),
("Decision analysis takes the set", """Generating alternatives is the weak point of decision support, and the mechanisms that address it share one pattern. Decision analysis takes the set of alternatives as given and concentrates on evaluating it {35;36}. Option generation has been identified as a neglected phase that precedes choice {18}, formalized as a recursion of decision problems {19;37}, and shown to decide adoption, since planning systems that hard-wire the open aspects of a problem are not used {20}. Cognitive evidence agrees. Option generation in ill-structured problems depends on ideation beyond memory retrieval, more options raise the quality of the best option {38}, and operators build courses of action from recognized patterns rather than from menus {23}. Hence, a DSS should generate alternatives during operation and filter them by a strict, external evaluation."""),
("Recent wildfire and disaster systems", """Recent wildfire and disaster systems add learned and generative components, but none opens the intervention space under physical verification. Dubey and Dubey {45} map a Transformer forecast to four fixed response levels through fifteen expert rules. Chandra et al. {46} draw actions from a fixed ontology and check them only for consistency. Cheng et al. {47} let LLMs weight a fixed set of indicators. Gerolimos et al. {48} call for open-world learning, but the openness concerns unseen event classes while the response stays a mapping to known actions. Multi-agent LLMs produce free-text plans whose hallucinations are contained by retrieval, output schemas, or post-hoc grading {49;50}. Cui et al. {51} argue that verification by physics-based simulation is required, yet confine their agent to a fixed tool library. WildfireKR {52}, the closest neighbour, automates calibrated spread simulation but leaves intervention design to the analyst. None of them builds new interventions from effects a physical model can execute and lets that model decide admission. This is the gap VODA fills."""),
]
